[
  {
    "id" : "consistency_exception_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Exception Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that exception names end with a common suffix like \"Error\" for consistency.",
    "explanation" : "Exception names should end with a common suffix like \"Error\".\n\nTry to name your exception modules consistently:\n\n    defmodule BadCodeError do\n      defexception [:message]\n    end\n\n    defmodule ParserError do\n      defexception [:message]\n    end\n\nInconsistent use should be avoided:\n\n    defmodule BadHTTPResponse do\n      defexception [:message]\n    end\n\n    defmodule HTTPHeaderException do\n      defexception [:message]\n    end\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_line_endings",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Line Endings",
    "description" : "Ensures that line endings are consistent across the codebase, avoiding mixing Windows and Linux/macOS styles.",
    "explanation" : "Windows and Linux/macOS systems use different line-endings in files.\n\nIt seems like a good idea not to mix these in the same codebase.\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "force",
        "default" : "null",
        "description" : "Force a choice, values can be `:unix` or `:windows`."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_multi_alias_import_require_use",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Multi Alias Import Style",
    "description" : "Ensures consistent usage of alias, import, require, or use statements when referencing multiple names from the same namespace.",
    "explanation" : "When using alias, import, require or use for multiple names from the same\nnamespace, you have two options:\n\nUse single instructions per name:\n\n    alias Ecto.Query\n    alias Ecto.Schema\n    alias Ecto.Multi\n\nor use one multi instruction per namespace:\n\n    alias Ecto.{Query, Schema, Multi}\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_parameter_pattern_matching",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Parameter Pattern Matching Style",
    "description" : "Ensures consistent usage of parameter pattern matching style within the codebase by enforcing either naming the parameter before or after the matched value.",
    "explanation" : "When capturing a parameter using pattern matching you can either put the parameter name before or after the value\ni.e.\n\n    def parse({:ok, values} = pair)\n\nor\n\n    def parse(pair = {:ok, values})\n\nNeither of these is better than the other, but it seems a good idea not to mix the two patterns in the same codebase.\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "force",
        "default" : "null",
        "description" : "Force a choice, values can be `:after` or `:before`."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_space_around_operators",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Spacing Around Operators",
    "description" : "Ensures consistent use of spaces around operators like +, -, *, and / in code.",
    "explanation" : "Use spaces around operators like `+`, `-`, `*` and `/`. This is the\n**preferred** way, although other styles are possible, as long as it is\napplied consistently.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    1 + 2 * 4\n\n    # also okay\n\n    1+2*4\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignore",
        "default" : "[\"|\"]",
        "description" : "List of operators to be ignored for this check."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_space_in_parentheses",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Spacing in Parentheses",
    "description" : "Ensures there are no spaces immediately after opening or before closing parentheses, brackets, or braces for consistent code style.",
    "explanation" : "Don't use spaces after `(`, `[`, and `{` or before `}`, `]`, and `)`. This is\nthe **preferred** way, although other styles are possible, as long as it is\napplied consistently.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    Helper.format({1, true, 2}, :my_atom)\n\n    # also okay\n\n    Helper.format( { 1, true, 2 }, :my_atom )\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allow_empty_enums",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allows [], %{} and similar empty enum values to be used regardless of spacing throughout the codebase."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_tabs_or_spaces",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Use of Tabs or Spaces",
    "description" : "Ensures that tabs or spaces are used consistently for indentation across all source files.",
    "explanation" : "Tabs should be used consistently.\n\nNOTE: This check does not verify the indentation depth, but checks whether\nor not soft/hard tabs are used consistently across all source files.\n\nIt is very common to use 2 spaces wide soft-tabs, but that is not a strict\nrequirement and you can use hard-tabs if you like that better.\n\nWhile this is not necessarily a concern for the correctness of your code,\nyou should use a consistent style throughout your codebase.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "force",
        "default" : "null",
        "description" : "Force a choice, values can be `:spaces` or `:tabs`."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "consistency_unused_variable_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Naming for Unused Variables",
    "description" : "This rule enforces a consistent style for naming unused variables in Elixir code, either using anonymous underscore `_` or meaningful underscore-prefixed names like `_user`.",
    "explanation" : "Elixir allows us to use `_` as a name for variables that are not meant to be\nused. But it’s a common practice to give these variables meaningful names\nanyway (`_user` instead of `_`), but some people prefer to name them all anonymously (`_`).\n\nA single style should be present in the same codebase.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "force",
        "default" : "null",
        "description" : "Force a choice, values can be `:meaningful` or `:anonymous`."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "design_alias_usage",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Design Alias Usage",
    "description" : "Functions from other modules should be used via an alias if the module's namespace is not top-level.",
    "explanation" : "Functions from other modules should be used via an alias if the module's\nnamespace is not top-level.\n\nWhile this is completely fine:\n\n    defmodule MyApp.Web.Search do\n      def twitter_mentions do\n        MyApp.External.TwitterAPI.search(...)\n      end\n    end\n\n... you might want to refactor it to look like this:\n\n    defmodule MyApp.Web.Search do\n      alias MyApp.External.TwitterAPI\n\n      def twitter_mentions do\n        TwitterAPI.search(...)\n      end\n    end\n\nThe thinking behind this is that you can see the dependencies of your module\nat a glance. So if you are attempting to build a medium to large project,\nthis can help you to get your boundaries/layers/contracts right.\n\nAs always: This is just a suggestion. Check the configuration options for\ntweaking or disabling this check.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "excluded_lastnames",
        "default" : "[\"Access\",\"Agent\",\"Application\",\"Atom\",\"Base\",\"Behaviour\",\"Bitwise\",\"Code\",\"Date\",\"DateTime\",\"Dict\",\"Enum\",\"Exception\",\"File\",\"Float\",\"GenEvent\",\"GenServer\",\"HashDict\",\"HashSet\",\"Integer\",\"IO\",\"Kernel\",\"Keyword\",\"List\",\"Macro\",\"Map\",\"MapSet\",\"Module\",\"NaiveDateTime\",\"Node\",\"OptionParser\",\"Path\",\"Port\",\"Process\",\"Protocol\",\"Range\",\"Record\",\"Regex\",\"Registry\",\"Set\",\"Stream\",\"String\",\"StringIO\",\"Supervisor\",\"System\",\"Task\",\"Time\",\"Tuple\",\"URI\",\"Version\"]",
        "description" : "List of lastnames to be excluded for this check."
      },
      {
        "name" : "excluded_namespaces",
        "default" : "[\"File\",\"IO\",\"Inspect\",\"Kernel\",\"Macro\",\"Supervisor\",\"Task\",\"Version\"]",
        "description" : "List of namespaces to be excluded for this check."
      },
      {
        "name" : "if_called_more_often_than",
        "default" : "0",
        "description" : "Only raise an issue if a module is called more often than this."
      },
      {
        "name" : "if_nested_deeper_than",
        "default" : "2",
        "description" : "Only raise an issue if a module is nested deeper than this."
      },
      {
        "name" : "if_referenced",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Raise an issue if a module is referenced by name, e.g. as an argument in a function call."
      },
      {
        "name" : "only",
        "default" : "null",
        "description" : "Regex or a list of regexes that specifies which modules to include for this check.\n\n`excluded_namespaces` and `excluded_lastnames` take precedence over this parameter.\n"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "design_duplicated_code",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Design Duplicated Code",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages copy-pasting code when it can be meaningfully abstracted to reduce duplication.",
    "explanation" : "Code should not be copy-pasted in a codebase when there is room to abstract\nthe copied functionality in a meaningful way.\n\nThat said, you should by no means \"ABSTRACT ALL THE THINGS!\".\n\nSometimes it can serve a purpose to have code be explicit in two places, even\nif it means the snippets are nearly identical. A good example for this are\nDatabase Adapters in a project like Ecto, where you might have nearly\nidentical functions for things like `order_by` or `limit` in both the\nPostgres and MySQL adapters.\n\nIn this case, introducing an `AbstractAdapter` just to avoid code duplication\nmight cause more trouble down the line than having a bit of duplicated code.\n\nLike all `Software Design` issues, this is just advice and might not be\napplicable to your project/situation.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "excluded_macros",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "List of macros to be excluded for this check."
      },
      {
        "name" : "mass_threshold",
        "default" : "40",
        "description" : "The minimum mass which a part of code has to have to qualify for this check."
      },
      {
        "name" : "nodes_threshold",
        "default" : "2",
        "description" : "The number of nodes that need to be found to raise an issue."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "design_skip_test_without_comment",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Require Comment for Skipped Tests",
    "description" : "Ensures that skipped tests have a preceding comment explaining why they are skipped.",
    "explanation" : "Skipped tests should have a comment documenting why the test is skipped.\n\nTests are often skipped using `@tag :skip` when some issue arises that renders\nthe test temporarily broken or unable to run. This temporary skip often becomes\na permanent one because the reason for the test being skipped is not documented.\n\nA comment should exist on the line prior to the skip tag describing why the test is\nskipped.\n\nExample:\n\n    # john: skipping this since our credentials expired, working on getting new ones\n    @tag :skip\n    test \"vendor api returns data\" do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nWhile the pure existence of a comment does not change anything per se, a thoughtful\ncomment can improve the odds for future iteration on the issue.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "files",
        "default" : "{\"included\":[\"test/**/*_test.exs\",\"apps/**/test/**/*_test.exs\"]}",
        "description" : null
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "design_tag_fixme",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid FIXME Comments",
    "description" : "Detects FIXME comments that indicate source code needing fixing.",
    "explanation" : "FIXME comments are used to indicate places where source code needs fixing.\n\nExample:\n\n    # FIXME: this does no longer work, research new API url\n    defp fun do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nThe premise here is that FIXME should indeed be fixed as soon as possible and\nare therefore reported by Credo.\n\nLike all `Software Design` issues, this is just advice and might not be\napplicable to your project/situation.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "include_doc",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to also include tags from @doc attributes."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "design_tag_todo",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid TODO Comments",
    "description" : "Detects TODO comments in source code used as reminders for future work.",
    "explanation" : "TODO comments are used to remind yourself of source code related things.\n\nExample:\n\n    # TODO: move this to a Helper module\n    defp fun do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nThe premise here is that TODO should be dealt with in the near future and\nare therefore reported by Credo.\n\nLike all `Software Design` issues, this is just advice and might not be\napplicable to your project/situation.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "include_doc",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to also include tags from @doc attributes."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_alias_as",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Alias Renaming with :as for Readability",
    "description" : "This rule discourages using the :as option to rename aliases in Elixir code, as it can reduce code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Aliases can be \"renamed\" using the `:as` option, but that sometimes\nmakes the code more difficult to read.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    def MyModule do\n      alias MyApp.Module1\n\n      def my_function(foo) do\n        Module1.run(foo)\n      end\n    end\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    def MyModule do\n      alias MyApp.Module1, as: M1\n\n      def my_function(foo) do\n        # what the heck is `M1`?\n        M1.run(foo)\n      end\n    end\n\nPlease note that you might want to deactivate this check for cases in which you have an alias that\nis used tons throughout your codebase.\n\nIf, for example, you are using a third-party module named `FlupsyTopsyDataRetentionServiceServer`\nin half your modules, it is of course reasonable to alias it to `Server`.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_alias_order",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Alphabetical Order of Aliases",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that alias declarations are alphabetically ordered to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Alphabetically ordered lists are more easily scannable by the reader.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    alias ModuleA\n    alias ModuleB\n    alias ModuleC\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    alias ModuleA\n    alias ModuleC\n    alias ModuleB\n\nAlias should be alphabetically ordered among their group:\n\n    # preferred\n\n    alias ModuleC\n    alias ModuleD\n\n    alias ModuleA\n    alias ModuleB\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    alias ModuleC\n    alias ModuleD\n\n    alias ModuleB\n    alias ModuleA\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "sort_method",
        "default" : "alpha",
        "description" : "The ordering method to use.\n\nOptions\n- `:alpha` - Alphabetical case-insensitive sorting.\n- `:ascii` - Case-sensitive sorting where upper case characters are ordered\n              before their lower case equivalent.\n"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_block_pipe",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Pipes with Blocks",
    "description" : "This rule discourages using the pipe operator (`|>`) with blocks to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Pipes (`|>`) should not be used with blocks.\n\nThe code in this example ...\n\n    list\n    |> Enum.take(5)\n    |> Enum.sort()\n    |> case do\n      [[_h | _t] | _] -> true\n      _ -> false\n    end\n\n... should be refactored to look like this:\n\n    maybe_nested_lists =\n      list\n      |> Enum.take(5)\n      |> Enum.sort()\n\n    case maybe_nested_lists do\n      [[_h | _t] | _] -> true\n      _ -> false\n    end\n\n... or create a new function:\n\n    list\n    |> Enum.take(5)\n    |> Enum.sort()\n    |> contains_nested_list?()\n\nPiping to blocks may be harder to read because it can be said that it obscures intentions\nand increases cognitive load on the reader. Instead, prefer introducing variables to your code or\nnew functions when it may be a sign that your function is getting too complicated and/or has too many concerns.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "exclude",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "Do not raise an issue for these macros and functions."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_function_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Snake Case for Function Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that function, macro, and guard names are written in snake_case in Elixir.",
    "explanation" : "Function, macro, and guard names are always written in snake_case in Elixir.\n\n    # snake_case\n\n    def handle_incoming_message(message) do\n    end\n\n    # not snake_case\n\n    def handleIncomingMessage(message) do\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allow_acronyms",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allows acronyms like HTTP or OTP in function names."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_impl_true",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using @impl true for Behaviour Implementations",
    "description" : "This rule discourages the use of the shorthand `@impl true` annotation when implementing behaviour callbacks, recommending specifying the actual behaviour instead for better readability and validation.",
    "explanation" : "`@impl true` is a shortform so you don't have to write the actual behaviour that is being implemented.\nThis can make code harder to comprehend.\n\n# preferred\n\n    @impl MyBehaviour\n    def my_funcion() do\n      # ...\n    end\n\n# NOT preferred\n\n    @impl true\n    def my_funcion() do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nWhen implementing behaviour callbacks, `@impl true` indicates that a function implements a callback, but\na more explicit way is to use the actual behaviour being implemented, for example `@impl MyBehaviour`.\n\nThis not only improves readability, but adds extra validation in cases where multiple behaviours are\nimplemented in a single module.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_large_numbers",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Readability of Large Numbers",
    "description" : "This rule encourages the use of underscores in large numeric literals to improve readability without changing their value.",
    "explanation" : "Numbers can contain underscores for readability purposes.\nThese do not affect the value of the number, but can help read large numbers\nmore easily.\n\n    141592654 # how large is this number?\n\n    141_592_654 # ah, it's in the hundreds of millions!\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "only_greater_than",
        "default" : "9999",
        "description" : "The check only reports numbers greater than this."
      },
      {
        "name" : "trailing_digits",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "The check allows for the given number of trailing digits (can be a number, range or list)"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_max_line_length",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Maximum Line Length",
    "description" : "This rule checks that lines in the code do not exceed a maximum length to enhance readability.",
    "explanation" : "Checks for the length of lines.\n\nIgnores function definitions and (multi-)line strings by default.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignore_definitions",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to ignore lines including function definitions."
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignore_heredocs",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : null
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignore_sigils",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to ignore lines that are sigils, e.g. regular expressions."
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignore_specs",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to ignore lines including `@spec`s."
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignore_strings",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to ignore lines that are strings or in heredocs."
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignore_urls",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `true` to ignore lines that contain urls."
      },
      {
        "name" : "max_length",
        "default" : "120",
        "description" : "The maximum number of characters a line may consist of."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_module_attribute_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Snake_Case for Module Attribute Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that module attribute names in Elixir are written in snake_case for readability.",
    "explanation" : "Module attribute names are always written in snake_case in Elixir.\n\n    # snake_case\n\n    @inbox_name \"incoming\"\n\n    # not snake_case\n\n    @inboxName \"incoming\"\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_module_doc",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Module Documentation",
    "description" : "Ensures every module contains comprehensive documentation or an explicit @moduledoc false to indicate no documentation is intended.",
    "explanation" : "Every module should contain comprehensive documentation.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    defmodule MyApp.Web.Search do\n      @moduledoc \"\"\"\n      This module provides a public API for all search queries originating\n      in the web layer.\n      \"\"\"\n    end\n\n    # also okay: explicitly say there is no documentation\n\n    defmodule MyApp.Web.Search do\n      @moduledoc false\n    end\n\nMany times a sentence or two in plain english, explaining why the module\nexists, will suffice. Documenting your train of thought this way will help\nboth your co-workers and your future-self.\n\nOther times you will want to elaborate even further and show some\nexamples of how the module's functions can and should be used.\n\nIn some cases however, you might not want to document things about a module,\ne.g. it is part of a private API inside your project. Since Elixir prefers\nexplicitness over implicit behaviour, you should \"tag\" these modules with\n\n    @moduledoc false\n\nto make it clear that there is no intention in documenting it.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignore_names",
        "default" : "[\"(\\\\.\\\\w+Controller|\\\\.Endpoint|\\\\.\\\\w+Live(\\\\.\\\\w+)?|\\\\.Repo|\\\\.Router|\\\\.\\\\w+Socket|\\\\.\\\\w+View|\\\\.\\\\w+HTML|\\\\.\\\\w+JSON|\\\\.Telemetry|\\\\.Layouts|\\\\.Mailer)$\"]",
        "description" : "All modules matching this regex (or list of regexes) will be ignored."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_module_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce PascalCase for Module Names",
    "description" : "Ensures that module names in Elixir are written using PascalCase convention.",
    "explanation" : "Module names are always written in PascalCase in Elixir.\n\n    # PascalCase\n\n    defmodule MyApp.WebSearchController do\n      # ...\n    end\n\n    # not PascalCase\n\n    defmodule MyApp.Web_searchController do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of other reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 1,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignore",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "List of ignored module names and patterns e.g. `[~r/Sample_Module/, \"Credo.Sample_Module\"]`"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_multi_alias",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Multi Alias Expansion for Readability",
    "description" : "This rule discourages using multi alias expansion in module uses to improve code readability and searchability in large code bases.",
    "explanation" : "Multi alias expansion makes module uses harder to search for in large code bases.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    alias Module.Foo\n    alias Module.Bar\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    alias Module.{Foo, Bar}\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_nested_function_calls",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Nested Function Calls",
    "description" : "This rule discourages nesting one function call inside another to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "A function call should not be nested inside another function call.\n\nSo while this is fine:\n\n    Enum.shuffle([1,2,3])\n\nThe code in this example ...\n\n    Enum.shuffle(Enum.uniq([1,2,3,3]))\n\n... should be refactored to look like this:\n\n    [1,2,3,3]\n    |> Enum.uniq()\n    |> Enum.shuffle()\n\nNested function calls make the code harder to read. Instead, break the\nfunction calls out into a pipeline.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "min_pipeline_length",
        "default" : "2",
        "description" : "Set a minimum pipeline length"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_one_arity_function_in_pipe",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Parentheses for One-Arity Functions in Pipe",
    "description" : "Ensures that one-arity functions used in a pipe operator (|>) are called with parentheses for better readability.",
    "explanation" : "Use parentheses for one-arity functions when using the pipe operator (|>).\n\n    # not preferred\n    some_string |> String.downcase |> String.trim\n\n    # preferred\n    some_string |> String.downcase() |> String.trim()\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 1,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_one_pipe_per_line",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce One Pipe Per Line in Pipe Chains",
    "description" : "Ensures that each function in a pipe chain is placed on its own line rather than having multiple pipes on the same line.",
    "explanation" : "Don't use multiple pipes (|>) in the same line.\nEach function in the pipe should be in it's own line.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    foo\n    |> bar()\n    |> baz()\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    foo |> bar() |> baz()\n\nThe code in this example ...\n\n    1 |> Integer.to_string() |> String.to_integer()\n\n... should be refactored to look like this:\n\n    1\n    |> Integer.to_string()\n    |> String.to_integer()\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_parentheses_in_condition",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Parentheses Around Conditions in if/unless",
    "description" : "Enforces omission of parentheses around conditions in if and unless statements for better readability.",
    "explanation" : "Because `if` and `unless` are macros, the preferred style is to not use\nparentheses around conditions.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    if valid?(username) do\n      # ...\n    end\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    if( valid?(username) ) do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_parentheses_on_zero_arity_defs",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consistent Use of Parentheses on Zero-Arity Function Definitions",
    "description" : "This rule enforces consistent use or omission of parentheses when defining functions or macros with zero arguments.",
    "explanation" : "Either use parentheses or not when defining a function with no arguments.\n\nBy default, this check enforces no parentheses, so zero-arity function\nand macro definitions should look like this:\n\n    def summer? do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nIf the `:parens` param is set to `true` for this check, then the check\nenforces zero-arity function and macro definitions to have parens:\n\n    def summer?() do\n      # ...\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "parens",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : null
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_pipe_into_anonymous_functions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Piping Into Anonymous Functions",
    "description" : "This rule discourages the use of piping into anonymous functions to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Avoid piping into anonymous functions.\n\nThe code in this example ...\n\n    def my_fun(foo) do\n      foo\n      |> (fn i -> i * 2 end).()\n      |> my_other_fun()\n    end\n\n... should be refactored to define a private function:\n\n    def my_fun(foo) do\n      foo\n      |> times_2()\n      |> my_other_fun()\n    end\n\n    defp timex_2(i), do: i * 2\n\n... or use `then/1`:\n\n    def my_fun(foo) do\n      foo\n      |> then(fn i -> i * 2 end)\n      |> my_other_fun()\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_predicate_function_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Readability of Predicate Function Names",
    "description" : "Ensures predicate functions and macros are named according to Elixir conventions for readability.",
    "explanation" : "Predicate functions/macros should be named accordingly:\n\n* For functions, they should end in a question mark.\n\n      # preferred\n\n      defp user?(cookie) do\n      end\n\n      defp has_attachment?(mail) do\n      end\n\n      # NOT preferred\n\n      defp is_user?(cookie) do\n      end\n\n      defp is_user(cookie) do\n      end\n\n* For guard-safe macros they should have the prefix `is_` and not end in a question mark.\n\n      # preferred\n\n      defmacro is_user(cookie) do\n      end\n\n      # NOT preferred\n\n      defmacro is_user?(cookie) do\n      end\n\n      defmacro user?(cookie) do\n      end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_prefer_implicit_try",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Prefer Implicit Try Over Explicit Try for Rescue",
    "description" : "Enforces using an implicit try/rescue block instead of an explicit try block when rescuing any exception in a function.",
    "explanation" : "Prefer using an implicit `try` rather than explicit `try` if you try to rescue\nanything the function does.\n\nFor example, this:\n\n    def failing_function(first) do\n      try do\n        to_string(first)\n      rescue\n        _ -> :rescued\n      end\n    end\n\nCan be rewritten without `try` as below:\n\n    def failing_function(first) do\n      to_string(first)\n    rescue\n      _ -> :rescued\n    end\n\nThis emphazises that you really want to try/rescue anything the function does,\nwhich might be important for other contributors so they can reason about adding\ncode to the function.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_redundant_blank_lines",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Redundant Consecutive Blank Lines",
    "description" : "This rule ensures that files do not contain two or more consecutive blank lines to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Files should not have two or more consecutive blank lines.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "max_blank_lines",
        "default" : "1",
        "description" : "The maximum number of tolerated consecutive blank lines."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_semicolons",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Semicolons to Separate Statements",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that statements and expressions should not be separated by semicolons but by new lines to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Don't use ; to separate statements and expressions.\nStatements and expressions should be separated by lines.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    a = 1\n    b = 2\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    a = 1; b = 2\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_separate_alias_require",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Consecutive Alias and Require Statements",
    "description" : "Ensures that all alias statements and all require statements are grouped consecutively within a file.",
    "explanation" : "All instances of `alias` should be consecutive within a file.\nLikewise, all instances of `require` should be consecutive within a file.\n\nFor example:\n\n    defmodule Foo do\n      require Logger\n      alias Foo.Bar\n\n      alias Foo.Baz\n      require Integer\n\n      # ...\n    end\n\nshould be changed to:\n\n    defmodule Foo do\n      require Integer\n      require Logger\n\n      alias Foo.Bar\n      alias Foo.Baz\n\n      # ...\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_single_function_to_block_pipe",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Single Pipe Into Blocks",
    "description" : "This rule discourages using a single pipe (`|>`) to pipe into blocks in Elixir code to improve readability.",
    "explanation" : "A single pipe (`|>`) should not be used to pipe into blocks.\n\nThe code in this example ...\n\n    list\n    |> length()\n    |> case do\n      0 -> :none\n      1 -> :one\n      _ -> :many\n    end\n\n... should be refactored to look like this:\n\n    case length(list) do\n      0 -> :none\n      1 -> :one\n      _ -> :many\n    end\n\nIf you want to disallow piping into blocks altogether, use\n`Credo.Check.Readability.BlockPipe`.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_single_pipe",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Single Pipe Operator Usage",
    "description" : "Enforces that the pipe operator (`|>`) should only be used when piping data through multiple function calls, not just one.",
    "explanation" : "Pipes (`|>`) should only be used when piping data through multiple calls.\n\nSo while this is fine:\n\n    list\n    |> Enum.take(5)\n    |> Enum.shuffle\n    |> evaluate()\n\nThe code in this example ...\n\n    list\n    |> evaluate()\n\n... should be refactored to look like this:\n\n    evaluate(list)\n\nUsing a single |> to invoke functions makes the code harder to read. Instead,\nwrite a function call when a pipeline is only one function long.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "allow_0_arity_functions",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Allow 0-arity functions"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_space_after_commas",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Readability by Using Space After Commas",
    "description" : "This rule enforces the use of white-space after commas in lists, tuples, and other enumerations to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "You can use white-space after commas to make items of lists,\ntuples and other enumerations easier to separate from one another.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    alias Project.{Alpha, Beta}\n\n    def some_func(first, second, third) do\n      list = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n      # ...\n    end\n\n    # NOT preferred - items are harder to separate\n\n    alias Project.{Alpha,Beta}\n\n    def some_func(first,second,third) do\n      list = [1,2,3,4,5]\n      # ...\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_specs",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Documentation",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Typespecs on Functions, Callbacks, and Macros",
    "description" : "Ensures that all functions, callbacks, and macros have typespec annotations.",
    "explanation" : "Functions, callbacks and macros need typespecs.\n\nAdding typespecs gives tools like Dialyzer more information when performing\nchecks for type errors in function calls and definitions.\n\n    @spec add(integer, integer) :: integer\n    def add(a, b), do: a + b\n\nFunctions with multiple arities need to have a spec defined for each arity:\n\n    @spec foo(integer) :: boolean\n    @spec foo(integer, integer) :: boolean\n    def foo(a), do: a > 0\n    def foo(a, b), do: a > b\n\nThe check only considers whether the specification is present, it doesn't\nperform any actual type checking.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "include_defp",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "Include private functions."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_strict_module_layout",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Strict Module Layout Order",
    "description" : "Ensures that the parts of a module are declared in a specific preferred order to improve readability.",
    "explanation" : "Provide module parts in a required order.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    defmodule MyMod do\n      @moduledoc \"moduledoc\"\n      use Foo\n      import Bar\n      alias Baz\n      require Qux\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignore",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "List of atoms identifying the module parts which are not checked, and may\ntherefore appear anywhere in the module. Supported values are the same as\nin the `:order` param.\n"
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignore_module_attributes",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "List of atoms identifying the module attributes which are not checked, and may\ntherefore appear anywhere in the module. Useful for custom DSLs that use attributes\nbefore function heads.\n\nFor example, if you provide `~w/trace/a`, all `@trace` attributes will be ignored.\n"
      },
      {
        "name" : "order",
        "default" : "[\"shortdoc\",\"moduledoc\",\"behaviour\",\"use\",\"import\",\"alias\",\"require\"]",
        "description" : "List of atoms identifying the desired order of module parts.\n\nSupported values are:\n\n- `:moduledoc` - `@moduledoc` module attribute\n- `:shortdoc` - `@shortdoc` module attribute\n- `:behaviour` - `@behaviour` module attribute\n- `:use` - `use` expression\n- `:import` - `import` expression\n- `:alias` - `alias` expression\n- `:require` - `require` expression\n- `:defstruct` - `defstruct` expression\n- `:opaque` - `@opaque` module attribute\n- `:type` - `@type` module attribute\n- `:typep` - `@typep` module attribute\n- `:callback` - `@callback` module attribute\n- `:macrocallback` - `@macrocallback` module attribute\n- `:optional_callbacks` - `@optional_callbacks` module attribute\n- `:module_attribute` - other module attribute\n- `:public_fun` - public function\n- `:private_fun` - private function or a public function marked with `@doc false`\n- `:public_macro` - public macro\n- `:private_macro` - private macro or a public macro marked with `@doc false`\n- `:callback_impl` - public function or macro marked with `@impl`\n- `:public_guard` - public guard\n- `:private_guard` - private guard or a public guard marked with `@doc false`\n- `:module` - inner module definition (`defmodule` expression inside a module)\n\nNotice that the desired order always starts from the top.\n\nFor example, if you provide the order `~w/public_fun private_fun/a`,\nit means that everything else (e.g. `@moduledoc`) must appear after\nfunction definitions.\n"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_string_sigils",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Use Readable String Sigils Instead of Escaped Quotes",
    "description" : "Encourages using string sigils for strings containing quotes to improve readability by avoiding excessive escaping.",
    "explanation" : "If you used quoted strings that contain quotes, you might want to consider\nswitching to the use of sigils instead.\n\n    # okay\n\n    \"<a href=\\\"http://elixirweekly.net\\\">#\\{text}</a>\"\n\n    # not okay, lots of escaped quotes\n\n    \"<a href=\\\"http://elixirweekly.net\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\">#\\{text}</a>\"\n\n    # refactor to\n\n    ~S(<a href=\"http://elixirweekly.net\" target=\"_blank\">#\\{text}</a>)\n\nThis allows us to remove the noise which results from the need to escape\nquotes within quotes.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "maximum_allowed_quotes",
        "default" : "3",
        "description" : "The maximum amount of escaped quotes you want to tolerate."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_trailing_blank_line",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Trailing Blank Line at End of File",
    "description" : "Ensures that files end with a trailing blank line (newline character).",
    "explanation" : "Files should end in a trailing blank line.\n\nThis is mostly for historical reasons: every text file should end with a \\n,\nor newline since this acts as `eol` or the end of the line character.\n\nSee also: http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_206\n\nMost text editors ensure this \"final newline\" automatically.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 1,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_trailing_white_space",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Trailing White Space",
    "description" : "Ensures there is no trailing white space (tabs or spaces) at the end of any line in the code.",
    "explanation" : "There should be no white-space (i.e. tabs, spaces) at the end of a line.\n\nMost text editors provide a way to remove them automatically.\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignore_strings",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "Set to `false` to check lines that are strings or in heredocs"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_unnecessary_alias_expansion",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Unnecessary Alias Expansion",
    "description" : "This rule checks for unnecessary use of braces when aliasing a single module, which reduces readability.",
    "explanation" : "Alias expansion is useful but when aliasing a single module,\nit can be harder to read with unnecessary braces.\n\n    # preferred\n\n    alias ModuleA.Foo\n    alias ModuleA.{Foo, Bar}\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    alias ModuleA.{Foo}\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_variable_names",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Variable Names in snake_case",
    "description" : "Ensures that all variable names in Elixir code are written in snake_case.",
    "explanation" : "Variable names are always written in snake_case in Elixir.\n\n    # snake_case:\n\n    incoming_result = handle_incoming_message(message)\n\n    # not snake_case\n\n    incomingResult = handle_incoming_message(message)\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 1,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_with_custom_tagged_tuple",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Custom Tags in 'with' Macros for Error Reporting",
    "description" : "This rule discourages the use of custom tagged tuples for error handling in 'with' macros to improve code readability.",
    "explanation" : "Avoid using custom tags for error reporting from `with` macros.\n\nThis code injects tuple_tag tags such as `:resource` and `:authz` for the purpose of error\nreporting.\n\n    with {:resource, {:ok, resource}} <- {:resource, Resource.fetch(user)},\n         {:authz, :ok} <- {:authz, Resource.authorize(resource, user)} do\n      do_something_with(resource)\n    else\n      {:resource, _} -> {:error, :not_found}\n      {:authz, _} -> {:error, :unauthorized}\n    end\n\nInstead, extract each validation into a separate helper function which returns error\ninformation immediately:\n\n    defp find_resource(user) do\n      with :error <- Resource.fetch(user), do: {:error, :not_found}\n    end\n\n    defp authorize(resource, user) do\n      with :error <- Resource.authorize(resource, user), do: {:error, :unauthorized}\n    end\n\nAt this point, the validation chain in `with` becomes clearer and easier to understand:\n\n    with {:ok, resource} <- find_resource(user),\n         :ok <- authorize(resource, user),\n         do: do_something(user)\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "readability_with_single_clause",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Prefer Case Over With for Single Clause with Else",
    "description" : "Enforces using `case` statements instead of `with` statements when there is a single pattern matching clause combined with an `else` branch for better readability.",
    "explanation" : "`with` statements are useful when you need to chain a sequence\nof pattern matches, stopping at the first one that fails.\n\nIf the `with` has a single pattern matching clause and no `else`\nbranch, it means that if the clause doesn't match than the whole\n`with` will return the value of that clause.\n\nHowever, if that `with` has also an `else` clause, then you're using `with` exactly\nlike a `case` and a `case` should be used instead.\n\nTake this code:\n\n    with {:ok, user} <- User.create(make_ref()) do\n      user\n    else\n      {:error, :db_down} ->\n        raise \"DB is down!\"\n\n      {:error, reason} ->\n        raise \"error: #{inspect(reason)}\"\n    end\n\nIt can be rewritten with a clearer use of `case`:\n\n    case User.create(make_ref()) do\n      {:ok, user} ->\n        user\n\n      {:error, :db_down} ->\n        raise \"DB is down!\"\n\n      {:error, reason} ->\n        raise \"error: #{inspect(reason)}\"\n    end\n\nLike all `Readability` issues, this one is not a technical concern.\nBut you can improve the odds of others reading and liking your code by making\nit easier to follow.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_abc_size",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Functions with High ABC Size",
    "description" : "This rule detects functions with a high ABC size metric, which is based on the number of assignments, branches, and conditions in the function.",
    "explanation" : "The ABC size describes a metric based on assignments, branches and conditions.\n\nA high ABC size is a hint that a function might be doing \"more\" than it\nshould.\n\nAs always: Take any metric with a grain of salt. Since this one was originally\nintroduced for C, C++ and Java, we still have to see whether or not this can\nbe a useful metric in a declarative language like Elixir.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "excluded_functions",
        "default" : "[]",
        "description" : "All functions listed will be ignored."
      },
      {
        "name" : "max_size",
        "default" : "30",
        "description" : "The maximum ABC size a function should have."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_append_single_item",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Append Single Item to Prepend and Reverse",
    "description" : "Encourages refactoring list construction by prepending items and reversing the list at the end instead of appending items to improve performance when building large lists.",
    "explanation" : "When building up large lists, it is faster to prepend than\nappend. Therefore: It is sometimes best to prepend to the list\nduring iteration and call Enum.reverse/1 at the end, as it is quite\nfast.\n\nExample:\n\n    list = list_so_far ++ [new_item]\n\n    # refactoring it like this can make the code faster:\n\n    list = [new_item] ++ list_so_far\n    # ...\n    Enum.reverse(list)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_apply",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Prefer Direct Function Calls Over apply/2 and apply/3",
    "description" : "Enforces calling functions directly when the number of arguments is known at compile time instead of using apply/2 or apply/3.",
    "explanation" : "Prefer calling functions directly if the number of arguments is known\nat compile time instead of using `apply/2` and `apply/3`.\n\nExample:\n\n    # preferred\n\n    fun.(arg_1, arg_2, ..., arg_n)\n\n    module.function(arg_1, arg_2, ..., arg_n)\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    apply(fun, [arg_1, arg_2, ..., arg_n])\n\n    apply(module, :function, [arg_1, arg_2, ..., arg_n])",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_cond_statements",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "Comprehensibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Minimum Branches in cond Statements",
    "description" : "Ensures that each cond statement has at least three branches, including the always true statement.",
    "explanation" : "Each cond statement should have 3 or more statements including the\n\"always true\" statement.\n\nConsider an `if`/`else` construct if there is only one condition and the\n\"always true\" statement, since it will more accessible to programmers\nnew to the codebase (and possibly new to Elixir).\n\nExample:\n\n    cond do\n      x == y -> 0\n      true -> 1\n    end\n\n    # should be written as\n\n    if x == y do\n      0\n    else\n      1\n    end",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_cyclomatic_complexity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Cyclomatic Complexity",
    "description" : "This rule detects functions with high cyclomatic complexity, indicating overly complex code that is prone to errors.",
    "explanation" : "Cyclomatic complexity (CC) is a software complexity metric closely\ncorrelated with coding errors.\n\nIf a function feels like it's gotten too complex, it more often than not also\nhas a high CC value. So, if anything, this is useful to convince team members\nand bosses of a need to refactor parts of the code based on \"objective\"\nmetrics.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "max_complexity",
        "default" : "9",
        "description" : "The maximum cyclomatic complexity a function should have."
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_double_boolean_negation",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Double Boolean Negation",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of double boolean negation (!!) which can obscure the original value of parameters.",
    "explanation" : "Having double negations in your code can obscure the parameter's original value.\n\n    # NOT preferred\n\n    !!var\n\nThis will return `false` for `false` and `nil`, and `true` for anything else.\n\nAt first this seems like an extra clever shorthand to cast anything truthy to\n`true` and anything non-truthy to `false`. But in most scenarios you want to\nbe explicit about your input parameters (because it is easier to reason about\nedge-cases, code-paths and tests).\nAlso: `nil` and `false` do mean two different things.\n\nA scenario where you want this kind of flexibility, however, is parsing\nexternal data, e.g. a third party JSON-API where a value is sometimes `null`\nand sometimes `false` and you want to normalize that before handing it down\nin your program.\n\nIn these case, you would be better off making the cast explicit by introducing\na helper function:\n\n    # preferred\n\n    defp present?(nil), do: false\n    defp present?(false), do: false\n    defp present?(_), do: true\n\nThis makes your code more explicit than relying on the implications of `!!`.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_filter_count",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Filter Count to Use Enum.count/2",
    "description" : "Detects and suggests refactoring instances where Enum.filter/2 followed by Enum.count/1 can be replaced by a single Enum.count/2 call for better performance.",
    "explanation" : "`Enum.count/2` is more efficient than `Enum.filter/2 |> Enum.count/1`.\n\nThis should be refactored:\n\n    [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n    |> Enum.filter(fn x -> rem(x, 3) == 0 end)\n    |> Enum.count()\n\nto look like this:\n\n    Enum.count([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], fn x -> rem(x, 3) == 0 end)\n\nThe reason for this is performance, because the two separate calls\nto `Enum.filter/2` and `Enum.count/1` require two iterations whereas\n`Enum.count/2` performs the same work in one pass.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_filter_filter",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Multiple Enum.filter Calls into One",
    "description" : "Detects and suggests refactoring of multiple consecutive Enum.filter/2 calls into a single Enum.filter/2 call with combined conditions for better performance.",
    "explanation" : "One `Enum.filter/2` is more efficient than `Enum.filter/2 |> Enum.filter/2`.\n\nThis should be refactored:\n\n    [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\n    |> Enum.filter(&String.contains?(&1, \"x\"))\n    |> Enum.filter(&String.contains?(&1, \"a\"))\n\nto look like this:\n\n    Enum.filter([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], fn letter ->\n      String.contains?(letter, \"x\") && String.contains?(letter, \"a\")\n    end)\n\nThe reason for this is performance, because the two separate calls\nto `Enum.filter/2` require two iterations whereas doing the\nfunctions in the single `Enum.filter/2` only requires one.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_filter_reject",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Filter and Reject to Single Filter",
    "description" : "Detects inefficient use of Enum.filter/2 followed by Enum.reject/2 and suggests combining them into a single Enum.filter/2 call for better performance.",
    "explanation" : "One `Enum.filter/2` is more efficient than `Enum.filter/2 |> Enum.reject/2`.\n\nThis should be refactored:\n\n    [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\n    |> Enum.filter(&String.contains?(&1, \"x\"))\n    |> Enum.reject(&String.contains?(&1, \"a\"))\n\nto look like this:\n\n    Enum.filter([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], fn letter ->\n      String.contains?(letter, \"x\") && !String.contains?(letter, \"a\")\n    end)\n\nThe reason for this is performance, because the two calls to\n`Enum.reject/2` and `Enum.filter/2` require two iterations whereas\ndoing the functions in the single `Enum.filter/2` only requires one.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "refactor_function_arity",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Complexity",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Refactor Function Arity",
    "description" : "Detects functions with too many parameters to improve code clarity and maintainability.",
    "explanation" : "A function can take as many parameters as needed, but even in a functional\nlanguage there can be too many parameters.\n\nCan optionally ignore private functions (check configuration options).",
    "enabled" : true,
